        fp: "Defendant wanted to break into an unoccupied retail electronics store at night to steal valuable tablets[cite: 53]. At 2:00 AM, Defendant approached the rear metal service door carrying an electric cordless grinder and a crowbar[cite: 53]. Finding the door secured by a heavy external padlock, Defendant used the grinder to cut the padlock shackle, removing it entirely[cite: 53]. Defendant then wedged the crowbar into the seam of the door and forced the spring latch open, swinging the door outward twelve inches[cite: 53]. Just as the door swung open, an exterior motion-activated security strobe light flashed and an audible siren began blaring[cite: 53]. Startled, Defendant immediately dropped the crowbar on the pavement, turned around, and ran down the alley without putting any part of his body or any tool across the threshold[cite: 53]. Defendant was arrested two blocks away[cite: 53]. The jurisdiction adheres strictly to traditional common law criminal definitions[cite: 53].",
