        fp: "A commercial electrical subcontractor entered into a written contract with an engineering prime contractor to install high-voltage switchgear for an industrial data center for $200,000, payable upon completion on October 1[cite: 53]. The contract contained the provision: 'Time is of the essence. No modification of this agreement shall be valid unless in a writing signed by both parties.'[cite: 53] On September 1, the subcontractor telephoned the prime contractor and stated: 'Due to custom busbar modifications requested by the data center owner, our installation will require an extra $15,000 and five additional days.'[cite: 53] The prime contractor orally replied: 'The extra $15,000 and five-day extension to October 6 are approved; proceed with the installation.'[cite: 53] Relying on this conversation, the subcontractor purchased the custom busbars and completed the work on October 5[cite: 53]. The prime contractor inspected and accepted the switchgear, but paid only $200,000, refusing to pay the additional $15,000 on the grounds that the oral modification was barred by the contract's no-oral-modification clause[cite: 53].",
