        fp: "An industrial tool manufacturing company designed and sold an electric table saw equipped with a plastic blade guard assembly[cite: 53]. The blade guard was fastened to the saw table by a thumbscrew mechanism[cite: 53]. Due to a design defect, the vibration of the motor during normal cutting operations caused the thumbscrew to rattle loose, causing the guard to tilt into the spinning 10-inch blade[cite: 53]. To prevent the guard from binding against the blade and slowing down production, a carpentry shop foreman permanently unbolted the entire guard assembly and threw it in the trash[cite: 53]. Four months later, a carpenter employed by the shop was cutting a plywood sheet[cite: 53]. The wood kicked back, and the carpenter's left hand slid across the unguarded blade, severing three fingers[cite: 53]. Uncontradicted expert testimony established that had the guard been attached, the carpenter's hand would not have contacted the blade[cite: 53]. The carpenter sued the saw manufacturer in strict products liability, alleging a defective guard latch design[cite: 53].",
