        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: Under Restatement (Third) of Torts: Products Liability § 4, a product manufacturer's compliance with an applicable government safety statute or administrative regulation is admissible in evidence as relevant to the safety of the product, but it is NOT conclusive on the issue of product defect[cite: 53]. Government safety standards establish a mandatory statutory floor (minimum standards), not a ceiling[cite: 53]. Compliance with safety regulations does not immunize a commercial manufacturer from strict products liability if the product was in fact defectively manufactured (e.g., an instantaneous chemical fuse flaw) and caused physical injury to consumers or foreseeable bystanders (Option B)[cite: 53]. Option A is incorrect because federal safety regulations in consumer products generally do not preempt state common law products liability claims unless express statutory preemption is enacted by Congress[cite: 53]. Option C is incorrect because regulatory compliance is admissible evidence of product standards, though not dispositive[cite: 53]. Option D is incorrect because regulatory compliance does not alter the burden of proof regarding manufacturing defects[cite: 53]."
