        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: A critical split of authority governs felony murder when a non-felon (such as a crime victim, police officer, or third-party bystander) fires the fatal shot[cite: 53]. Under the majority Agency Theory of felony murder, the felony murder rule applies ONLY IF the lethal act is committed by one of the felons or an agent acting in furtherance of the common criminal conspiracy[cite: 53]. When an innocent neighbor or victim shoots and kills a co-felon in lawful self-defense, the surviving felon cannot be convicted of felony murder because the fatal shot was fired by an adversary rather than an agent of the felony (the landmark State v. Canola rule)[cite: 53]. Because this jurisdiction explicitly adheres to the agency theory, Defendant cannot be convicted of felony murder for the death of his accomplice (Option B)[cite: 53]. Options A and C are incorrect because they describe the proximate cause theory, which this jurisdiction rejects[cite: 53]. Option D is incorrect because vacation or unoccupied residential homes used for regular habitation qualify as dwellings at common law; the bar arises from the agency theory[cite: 53]."
