        exp: "Rule: In a pure comparative negligence jurisdiction, a plaintiff's contributory fault does not completely bar recovery[cite: 53]. Instead, the plaintiff's damages are reduced in direct proportion to the percentage of fault attributed to the plaintiff by the trier of fact[cite: 53]. Here, the customer suffered $50,000 in damages and was found 40% at fault[cite: 53]. His recovery is reduced by 40% ($20,000), leaving an enforceable award of $30,000 against the boutique (Option C)[cite: 53]. Option A is incorrect because pure comparative fault abolishes the traditional common law rule that any contributory negligence completely bars recovery[cite: 53]. Option B is incorrect because recovery is reduced by the plaintiff's own percentage of fault, not by the differential between the parties' fault shares[cite: 53]. Option D is incorrect because business owners are not strictly liable insurers; the plaintiff's comparative fault operates to reduce the total award[cite: 53]."
